# ADDED BY THE SVAR PRODUCTION PLANNER PROJECT.
# NOT part of the upstream SVAR sources.
#
# SVAR-LOCAL-ASSETS: repository-wide LF normalization for text files.
#
# Independently demonstrated, not assumed: on a checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (a common Windows Git default — this project's own
# workstations are not all configured the same way), planner-assets/fonts/
# OFL-OpenSans.txt and OFL-Roboto.txt check out as CRLF, while the sha256
# values tools/planner-fonts.mjs verifies them against were recorded from the
# LF bytes actually committed (`git show HEAD:<path>` — the blob itself is
# LF-only; only the working-tree checkout varied). That mismatch fails the
# build closed, which is correct behaviour for a corrupted file but wrong here
# — the file was never corrupted, only reformatted by checkout. The same class
# of drift reaches `.jsx` sources too: their bytes end up inside the shipped
# sourcemap (`dist/index.es.js.map`) as `sourcesContent`, and a checkout that
# silently changed them changes what that map records, even though it does
# not change what `dist/index.es.js` executes.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes every text file in this repository normalize to LF
# in the index and check out as LF regardless of `core.autocrlf`, so the same
# commit produces the same working-tree bytes on every machine. It does NOT
# by itself explain or fix the sourcemap `sources`-path non-determinism fixed
# in vite.config.js (`sourcemapPathTransform`) — that was a different defect,
# caused by an ephemeral install-time directory path leaking into the map, not
# by line endings; measured directly, this machine's own default git
# configuration already checked those particular files out as LF with no
# CRLF present, and the map hash still varied between installs until the
# separate fix. The two are independent hardenings, kept in the same commit
# only because both touch the identity of the same shipped sourcemap.
#
# The `binary` rules below are unaffected: git attribute precedence gives the
# LAST matching pattern the win for a given path, so these more specific,
# later rules keep the font binaries marked `binary` (which also implies
# `-text`) regardless of the blanket rule above them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The web fonts under planner-assets/fonts/ are checked against recorded
# sha256 values at build time (tools/planner-fonts.mjs), so a checkout that
# altered a single byte would stop the build rather than ship a broken face.
# Git's own binary detection already prevents that, but this project builds
# the same commit on Windows workstations where core.autocrlf is commonly on,
# and a guarantee worth having is worth stating rather than inheriting from a
# heuristic.
planner-assets/fonts/*.woff  binary
planner-assets/fonts/*.woff2 binary
